WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:18.480
JAPAN’S PEAK:
On December 29, 1989, Japan’s stock market

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.189
index reached a value of 38,957 points.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:29.529
From there, the index saw a decent sell off
of about 30% over the next couple of months.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:34.420
Not much was thought of this at the time given
that there was a global market sell off.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.480
So, most assumed that this was just a routine
bear market.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.700
But, the bear market quickly became much more
painful.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:49.770
Within 9 months after the peak, the index
would crash all the way down to 19,760 points

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.760
which correlates to about a 50% sell off.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.250
By this point, it was clear that this was
no longer just a routine bear market.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.850
This was now a monumental crash similar to
the dotcom crash or the 2008 financial recession

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.580
that people would remember for decades.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.009
Fortunately from there, it seemed like the
bottom was in.

00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:14.760
The index would bounce a solid 37% and no
new lows would be formed for well over a year.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.930
But, if you invested during this time period,
you would soon be regretting it because the

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:24.780
index would make yet another leg down by the
end of 1992, falling a total of 63%.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.579
If I was a Japanese citizen during this time
period, I would’ve almost definitely been

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:31.579
all in.

00:01:31.579 --> 00:01:35.549
And every month, I’d probably be chucking
my entire paycheck at the index, thinking

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:39.320
that it was just a matter of time until this
thing recovered.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.040
And that’s what it seemed like for a very
long time.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:48.049
For the next 8 years, the index would bounce
between 13,000 and 23,000 points and seemingly

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:49.930
built a massive base.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:54.150
Surely, this was the base that was required
to support the next bull market.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.780
But, the exact opposite would take place once
again.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.969
At the turn of the century, the dotcom bubble
would burst which push the index all the way

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:05.360
down to 7500 points.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.800
This was no longer just a stock market crash,
this was a full on stock market depression

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.140
comparable to that of the great depression.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.319
But, even this was not the bottom.

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:20.849
The real bottom wouldn’t come till November
of 2008 when the index crashed a total of

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:23.040
82%.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.519
Since then, the index has recovered substantially
growing over 300%, but even today, we have

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:34.720
yet to surpass the all time high that was
set nearly 33 years ago and that’s with

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:35.760
inflation.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.519
And with the global economy entering yet another
downturn, it doesn’t seem like Japan will

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:45.260
be making any new all time highs in the imminent
future.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.760
This doesn’t just apply to the Japanese
stock market either.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:52.710
If we switch over to the real estate market,
we’ll see that a very similar trend has

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.030
materialized.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:03:00.379
After decades of growth, the real estate market
peaked out in 1991 before crashing 50%.

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:03.959
And even today, the market is down about 30%.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:08.400
If we take a look at their GDP numbers, we’ll
see a slightly better picture.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.800
Instead of going straight down and not recovering,
their GDP has more or less gone sideways for

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.940
30 years.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:19.129
But, for a country like Japan that was once
universally known for innovation and progress,

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:21.030
this too is disappointing.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:29.220
So, what in the world happened to Japan and
is this what’s eventually in store for America?

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.950
UNPRECEDENTED GROWTH:
Even though the Japanese stock market didn’t

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:38.730
peak out till the end of the 80s, the seeds
for devastation had been planted decades earlier.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.019
It all started right after WW2.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:47.630
The war had destroyed Japan in every sense,
physically, economically, mentally, spiritually,

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:48.630
you name it.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:53.319
The Japanese empire had diverted almost everything
they had to the war effort for several years,

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:56.090
and all they had to show for it was pure devastation.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:00.530
And to put salt on the wound, there were nearly
a million American soldiers occupying their

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:01.720
largest cities.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:07.260
But, while morale was at all time lows, something
was changing behind the scenes.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.060
The reason America was occupying Japan was
to make sure that Japan didn’t try to pull

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:12.640
a quick on and start another conflict.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.009
In other words, America was basically trying
to babysit Japan, but the dynamics of this

00:04:18.009 --> 00:04:20.660
relationship rapidly evolved into being a
partnership.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.300
You see, it didn’t take long for America
to realize that Japan wasn’t the biggest

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.320
threat.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.740
The biggest threat was actually the soviet
union, and it was in America’s best interest

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:32.250
to have a partner in the east.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:37.020
The only two viable candidates for this role
were of course Japan and China.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.800
And given that China was barreling towards
a communist revolution, America had to choose

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:42.800
Japan.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.470
So, over the next several years, they worked
with Japan to establish a western capitalist

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.470
economy.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:52.430
General Douglas MacArthur worked with Japanese
leaders to implement a new constitution that

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:56.850
prioritized a limited government, free and
fair elections, private property, and individual

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:57.850
liberties.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:02.960
They also eased off on the ridiculous income
tax that went up to 86%.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:08.000
These changes set up Japan for success, and
after the Americans left in the early 1950s,

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.460
Japan was off to the races.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:16.380
Between 1950 and 1973, Japan would grow at
twice the rate of Western Europe and 2.5 times

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.030
the rate of America during the 1960s.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:23.400
This resulted in their stock market exploding
a mind boggling 6100%.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:30.130
While this is no doubt extraordinary, one
caveat that we should note is that Japan was

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:31.919
growing from nearly zero.

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:36.850
So, they had a lot more headroom to grow than
traditional western countries, and such growth

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.410
was obviously not sustainable indefinitely.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:46.250
But, Japan wanted to make it indefinite and
this is what would eventually seal their fate.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:51.870
THE PLAZA ACCORD:
As you might’ve guessed, the main way in

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:55.289
which Japan tried to keep the growth going
was through money printing.

00:05:55.289 --> 00:06:00.240
But, we can’t blame them too much because
growth wasn’t the only reason that Japan

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.289
was printing money.

00:06:01.289 --> 00:06:06.870
Japan’s choice to print money was actually
overseen and backed by all of the most prominent

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.360
western countries through an agreement called
the Plaza Accord.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:14.970
The Plaza Accord was an agreement between
the G-5 nations, and was actually designed

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.099
to help Japan and Germany.

00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:22.470
You see, while the Japanese and German economies
had grown exponentially since WW2, there was

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:26.460
still a massive trade imbalance between these
countries and the United States.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.440
The main reason for this was the rapid appreciation
of the US dollar in the 1980s.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:36.790
After a decade of high inflation in the 1970s,
the US finally put their foot down and jacked

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:38.660
up rates all the way to 20%.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:43.010
While this move was effective in cooling down
inflation and helping the dollar appreciate

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:49.060
47.9%, this move also had some unintended
consequences like the trade imbalance.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:53.430
A strong dollar meant that it was extremely
expensive for foreign countries like Japan

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.849
and Germany to buy US goods.

00:06:55.849 --> 00:07:01.199
And for companies with a large international
presence, this was terrible news, so they

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:06.910
decided to lobby congress to weaken the dollar
which eventually resulted in the Plaza Accord.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:11.770
Instead of focusing on growth, the US agreed
to focus on addressing their budget deficit.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.440
Meanwhile, Germany and Japan agreed to cut
taxes, lower interest rates, and of course

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.550
print money to hopefully stimulate their economies.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:25.020
This plan actually worked out surprisingly
well as the US dollar fell as much as 25.8%

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.139
within the next two years.

00:07:27.139 --> 00:07:32.280
This nearly eliminated the trade deficit between
the US and Germany, but the deficit between

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.110
Japan and the US was still non-negligible.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:40.590
Nonetheless, the US didn’t want to overdo
the weakening of the dollar, so they would

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.379
implement another accord called the Louvre
Accord.

00:07:43.379 --> 00:07:47.509
This accord was meant to essentially reverse
all of the policies set forth by the Plaza

00:07:47.509 --> 00:07:49.819
Accord and stabilize exchange rates.

00:07:49.819 --> 00:07:54.629
But even though the Louvre accord was signed
rather proactively, it seems that Japan had

00:07:54.629 --> 00:08:01.370
already overdone the economic loosening as
asset prices had way too far.

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:07.099
MONSTROUS VALUATIONS:
A lot of people like to claim that the overvalution

00:08:07.099 --> 00:08:12.130
of the US stock market in 2020 and 2021 was
the worst that we’ve seen.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:18.660
But, in reality, that was nothing in comparison
to the Japanese stock market in the late 1980s.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:20.960
Take the price to earnings ratio for example.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.810
The median for the S&amp;P 500 over the past 90
years is 14.90.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:32.140
In 2020 and 2021, this ratio reached levels
of 35 to 40, so there’s no doubt the US

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:36.940
stock market was overvalued, and it’s just
now starting to return to normal levels.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:40.529
But, do you what levels the Japanese stock
market reached?

00:08:40.529 --> 00:08:44.110
Well, it reached PE ratios of 60.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:49.959
For perspective, the US only reached PE ratios
of 44 even during the Dotcom bubble.

00:08:49.959 --> 00:08:56.111
The US did reach PE ratios as high 123 during
the financial crisis, but that wasn’t due

00:08:56.111 --> 00:08:58.610
to asset valuations getting super high.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:03.130
It was actually due to corporate earnings
falling off a cliff which screwed up the ratio.

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:05.670
This wasn’t what was happening in Japan.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:09.110
They straight up just had a PE ratio of 60.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:13.720
This meant that their stock market would have
to crash 75% just to return to normal levels.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:18.290
A very similar trend was beginning appear
within the real estate market as well.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:23.110
By 1985, Tokyo’s commercial districts had
already reached full capacity.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:27.760
This meant that if you wanted a piece of the
pie, you had to buy it from someone else.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.730
And given that the economy was booming, there
wasn’t very many people who were willing

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:32.930
to sell.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:36.180
This quickly resulted in buyers becoming desperate.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.760
Combine this with record low interest rates,
and it didn’t take long for real estate

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.230
to turn into one massive bidding war.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:47.180
In 1984, one square meter of land in Tokyo’s
commercial district cost $5,600.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:52.690
By 1985, this rose to $7,958 per square meter.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:57.281
As you can see, prices were already skyrocketing,
and when you threw the Plaza accord into the

00:09:57.281 --> 00:09:59.270
mix, things went parabolic.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:04.070
By 1986, a single square meter of commercial
land cost $25,000.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:08.970
By 1987, this rose to $45,000.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:15.680
And by 1989, we were talking about an eye
watering $218,000 per square meter.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.589
For my fellow metrically disabled peers, that
translates to $20,000 per square feet.

00:10:20.589 --> 00:10:25.790
Certain parts of Tokyo had appreciated 40X
in a matter of 5 years.

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:30.230
And at this point, it was just a matter of
time until valuations collapsed.

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:34.560
To make things worse, Japan was also starting
to undergo a demographics crisis.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:39.200
As countries develop, birth rates tend to
go down, and eventually, they become so low

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.670
that the country is no longer self sustaining.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:45.940
This has actually happened to the US as well,
but thanks to America’s relatively generous

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:49.639
immigration policies, the country has been
able to continue growing.

00:10:49.639 --> 00:10:54.630
Japan’s immigration policies, however, have
never been as leniant as America’s, and

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:59.269
many experts even go as far as claiming that
Japan’s immigration policies are self destructive.

00:10:59.269 --> 00:11:04.279
And when you combine declining demographics
with ludicrous asset valuations, Japan is

00:11:04.279 --> 00:11:06.529
what you get.

00:11:06.529 --> 00:11:11.899
THE LOST DECADES:
This monumental decline in Japan was initially

00:11:11.899 --> 00:11:17.360
dubbed the lost decade, but it has since been
revised to be named the lost decades.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.470
And honestly, there’s no sugar coating it.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:25.810
Japan’s economy has stagnated for 3 decades,
and with the current economic decline, it

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.990
seems highly likely that that will turn into
4 decades.

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:32.610
Japan’s financial situation doesn’t make
things any better.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:37.940
Japan is the most indebted country in the
world relative to their GDP coming in a painful

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:40.170
237%.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:45.990
Even the US with our ridiculous $31 trillion
is only at 125% or about half of that of Japan.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:49.620
But, honestly none of that is even Japan’s
biggest concern.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:55.750
By far Japan’s biggest concern is their
waning demographics. 28.7% of Japan’s population

00:11:55.750 --> 00:12:02.820
is over the age of 65 and only 12.3% are under
the age of 15, so in terms of young ambitious

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.310
souls, they don’t have very many.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:09.740
We should also mention that their migrant
population has also been on the decline.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:14.190
And without a positive change in their demographic
profile or some sort of innovative miracle,

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:19.960
I just don’t see how Japan can enter another
highly prosperous period anytime soon.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:24.790
As for how this applies to the US, given that
our markets are almost back to normal levels,

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:29.050
and given that our immigration numbers are
still relatively strong, I don’t think we’re

00:12:29.050 --> 00:12:34.480
headed into some sort of 30 to 40 year long
bear market, but that’s just what I think.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:37.839
When do you think this current economic decline
will come to a close?

00:12:37.839 --> 00:12:39.029
Comment that down below.

00:12:39.029 --> 00:12:43.250
Also, drop a like if you hope that your country
doesn’t end up like Japan.

